## A B S T R A C T
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) have become premium candidates for the removal of hazardous contaminants from wastewater. However, MOFs have a vast obstacle which is their poor recyclability. In this study, ZIF-67 was decorated with magnetic $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles, and then embedded into aminated chitosan (AmCs) matrix to form core-dual shell $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{ZIF-67}@\mathrm{AmCs}$ composite beads. Diverse analysis tools were utilized to ensure the suc­ cessful fabrication of the magnetic composite beads. The fabricated magnetic composite beads were examined their adsorptive removal aptitude towards toxic $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ ions. The gained results refereed that a maximum adsorption capacity of $119.05~\mathrm{mg/g}$ was attained by magnetic $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{ZIF-67}@\mathrm{AmCs}$ composite beads at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ The process obeyed both of Langmuir and Freundlich isotherm models, and the pseudo 2nd order was more suitable kinetic model to represent the adsorption process. Besides, $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{ZIF-67}@\mathrm{AmCs}$ composite showed an excellent recyclability for the removal of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ ions from their aqueous solutions for seven consecutive cycles.
## 1. Introduction
Water pollution has become the most serious affair worldwide as the unsafe water is fiercer on humanity than wars. Notably, hexavalent chromium $(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI}))$ is considered as the most notorious contaminant due to its toxicity, non-degradability as well as teratogenic, mutagenic and carcinogenic effects (Li, Dong et al., 2020; Mohamed et al., 2017). Consequently, $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ has been assorted in group 1 (carcinogenic to humans) by the International Agency for Research on Cancer (Dehghani, Sanaei, Ali, & Bhatnagar, 2016). However, vital industries depend on Cr (VI) such as; steel, catalysis, cement, textile, fungicide, electroplating and dyeing (Omer, Tamer et al., 2020; Wei et al., 2019). Thus, it is a crucial issue to develop efficient treatment techniques for the removal of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ from wastewater. Although many techniques have been utilized for this purpose such as electrocoagulation (Verma, Dash, & Bhunia, 2012), Fenton oxidation (Ganzenko et al., 2018), catalytic reduction (El-Subruiti, Eltaweil, & Sallam, 2019; Sallam, El-Subruiti, & Eltaweil,
2018), membrane system (Gu et al., 2018) and photo-catalysis (Ahmadi et al., 2017), adsorption technique is still more favorable as it is an easy, economical and high effective approach (Abbasi et al., 2016; El Bestawy, El-Shatby, & Eltaweil, 2020; Eltaweil, Abd El-Monaem, Omer et al., 2020; Omer, Elgarhy, El-Subruiti, Khalifa, & Eltaweil, 2020). So, looking up a new low cost adsorbent with high adsorption capacity and high renewability is a significant challenge.
In the last decades, to extend the scale of adsorbent selection, a novel category of porous material has been extensively developed which is Metal Organic Frameworks (MOFs). Due to the unparalleled merits of MOFs including the presence of in-pore functionality, water stability, good chemical and thermal stability, high surface area and tunable pore size, they have been widely utilized in enormous fields such as; gas storage, catalysis, drug delivery and especially in wastewater treatment (Du et al., 2017; Eltaweil, Elshishini, Ghatass, & Elsubruiti, 2021; Yuwei & Jianlong, 2011). Zeolitic Imidazole Frameworks (ZIFs), a promising subset of MOFs that is crafted from divalent metal ions and imidazolate ligands (Li, Wang et al., 2020; Mahmoodi, Oveisi, Taghizadeh, & Taghizadeh, 2020), has received a great deal of interest from the re­ searchers owing to its hydrophobic surface, high chemical and thermal stability, large surface area and water stability (Chiang et al., 2019; Khandelwal, Maria Joseph Raj, & Kim, 2020; Wang, Li et al., 2019). Based on these mentioned properties, ZIFs could be excellent adsorbents for the removal of Cr(VI) from aqueous solution. Unfortunately, MOFs generally suffer from significant drawbacks such as poor recyclability and difficult separation. Therefore, further researchers have been striving to overcome these obstacles (Zhao et al., 2020). One feasible solution is the formation of a magnetic composite that facilitates a perfect separation for the adsorbent by an external magnet instead of traditional approaches (i.e. filtration, centrifugation, etc.) (Abd El-Monaem, El-Latif, Mona, Eltaweil, & El-Subruiti, 2021; Travlou, Kyzas, Lazaridis, & Deliyanni, 2013). Besides, modification of MOF by a formable material in varies forms including beads, membranes, fibers, etc. is an excellent solution with multi-benefits including easy separa­ tion, decent reusability as well as better adsorption profiles (Eltaweil, Elgarhy, El-Subruiti, & Omer, 2020; Sun et al., 2019).
One of the most biopolymers that drawn much interest in recent years as an efficient adsorbent is chitosan (Cs) owing to its special fea­ tures including biocompatibility, hydrophilicity, biodegradability, adhesion properties, recyclability as well as polyelectrolyte properties (Fan, Luo, Sun, Li, & Qiu, 2013; Yuwei & Jianlong, 2011). Chitosan can be produced by N-deacetylation of chitin that extracts from accessible sources especially crustaceans such as shrimp, insects, crabs, fungi, etc. (Massoudinejad, Rasoulzadeh, & Ghaderpoori, 2019; Travlou, Kyzas, Lazaridis, & Deliyanni, 2013). In addition, chitosan structure possesses abundance of chemically active hydroxyl and amino groups that facili­ tate its functionalization via polymer-grafting, $N\mathrm{.}$ and O-hydroxylation, carboxymethylation, and amination to obtain chitosan-derivatives with noteworthy properties (Eltaweil, El-Tawil, El-Monaem, Eman, & El-Subruiti, 2021; Eldin et al., 2015; Kyzas & Bikiaris, 2015; Omer, Ahmed, El-Subruiti, Khalifa, & Eltaweil, 2021). All these special merits of chitosan make it a good candidate to remove bountiful contaminants from aqueous solution (Asiabi, Mehdinia, & Jabbari, 2017; Shebl, Omer, & Tamer, 2018; Upadhyay, Sreedhar, Singh, Patel, & Anitha, 2020). The respectable thermodynamic profiles of chitosan and MOF based-adsorbents (Annadurai, Ling, & Lee, 2008; Zhang, Zhang, Liu, Xiong, & Chen, 2016) nominate their combination with accepted ther­ modynamic compatibility. The compatibility of their mixtures could be controlled by several parameters such as composition ratios, reaction time and temperature (Rana, Bag, Bhattacharyya, & Mandal, 2000; Rana, Mandal, & Bhattacharyya, 1993, 1996a, 1996b). Therefore, chitosan-ZIF-67 based-adsorbents are anticipated to have multi-features including respectable reusability, easy separation property, and high adsorptive removal percentages.
Herein, we focused on modifying ZIF-67 by fabricating novel mag­ netic MOF-aminated chitosan beads $(\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{ZIF-67}@\mathrm{AmCs})$ . The com­ bination of both $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{m}\mathbf{C}s$ with ZIF-67 possess dual function, since they could ameliorate the adsorption property of ZIF-67. In addi­ tion, the easy molding of AmCs is expected to facilitate the MOF shaping, while the magnetic property of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ could provide a perfect separation to $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{ZIF-67}@\mathrm{AmCs}$ composite beads. The fabricated core- dual shell $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F-67}@\mathrm{AmCs}$ composite beads were utterly characterized by different tools. Besides, the efficiency of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{ZIF}{-}67@\mathrm{AmCs}$ com­ posite beads towards the removal of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ ions from their aqueous solutions was assessed utilizing a batch adsorption technique. Further­ more, the kinetics and isotherms were studied as well as the recyclability test of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{ZIF-67}@\mathrm{AmCs}$ beads was tested and discussed.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Materials
Chitin, 2-methylimidazole (MeIm), potassium dichromate $(\mathrm{K_{2}C r_{2}O_{7}})$ , $p$ -Benzoquinone (PBQ; $99\%$ ), ethylenediamine (EDA; $99\%$ ) were provided from Sigma-Aldrich (Germany). Sodium hydroxide (99 $\%)$ , $^{N,N}$ -dimethyl formamide $(99\%)$ ), ethanol $(99\%)$ and methanol (99 $\%)$ were purchased from Alpha Chemika (India). Hydrochloric acid (37 $\%)$ , ammonium hydroxide $(99\%)$ and acetic acid $(98~\%)$ were supplied from Aladdin Reagent Co., Ltd (China). Ferric chloride hexahydrate $(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0)$ , Cobalt(II) nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ and ferrous chloride tetrahydrate $\mathrm{(FeCl_{2}{\cdot}4H_{2}O)}$ were obtained from MP Biomedicals, LLC (France).
## 2.2. Preparation of $F e_{3}O_{4}$ nanoparticles
$\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles were fabricated via co-precipitation method (Fan et al., 2013; Massoudinejad et al., 2019); typically, $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and $\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ with molar ratio 1:2 were dissolved into $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ deionized water in a three-necked flask under $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ -atmosphere. After $10\ \mathrm{min}$ , $\mathrm{\DeltaNH_{4}O H}$ $25~\%)$ was slowly added until $\mathrm{pH}\sim10\$ , and then the mixture solution was stirred for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Finally, the product was separated by an external magnet, washed with deionized water and ethanol and dried in vacuum oven at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
## 2.3. Preparation of ZIF-67
ZIF-67 was fabricated via solvothermal method (Eltaweil, Mohamed, Abd El-Monaem, & El-Subruiti, 2020); in brief, $0.425\mathrm{~g~Co(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ and $0.4670{\mathrm g}$ MeIm were dissolved into $30~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF under gentle stirring for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . The reaction solution was transferred into a $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ auto­ clave and heated at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The resultant violet powder was separated by centrifugation, washed with DMF and methanol and dried in oven at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight.
## 2.4. Preparation of aminated chitosan (AmCs)
AmCs was fabricated according to the authors previous work (Liang et al., 2019); chitin was converted to AmCs as follow; exactly, $_{8\mathrm{~g~}}$ chitin was added to an aqueous solution of PBQ ( $\mathrm{100~mL}$ , $6.9\ \mathrm{mM}$ , then adjusted the solution $\mathsf{p H}\sim9$ and stirred at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The obtained PBQ-chitin powder was separated by centrifugation and washed with distilled $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ to eliminate the un-reacted PBQ. After that, PBQ-chitin powder was suspended in an aqueous solution of EDA $\mathrm{2100~mL}$ , $6.9\ \mathrm{mM}$ ) under constant stirring at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ , then the resultant aminated chitin was collected by centrifugation and washed with distilled $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ to eliminate the un-reacted EDA. Finally, AmCs was pro­ duced via deacetylation of the aminated chitin utilizing $50\%\mathrm{NaOH}$ with constant stirring at $130^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . After $24\mathrm{h}$ , the resultant AmCs was collected by centrifugation, repeatedly washed with distilled $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ until $\mathrm{pH}\sim7$ and dried in an oven at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight. The quantity of amine groups in the prepared AmCs was estimated as a function of ion exchange capacity measurement, which was recorded as $5.9\mathrm{\meq/g}$ (Tamer et al., 2017).
## 2.5. Fabrication of magnetic $F e_{3}O_{4},$ /ZIF-67@AmCs composite beads
A certain amount of AmCs was solubilized into $25~\mathrm{mL}$ acetic acid solution $(5\ \%\ \mathrm{wt/v})$ under vigorous stirring for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ . In another container, an appropriate amount of ZIF-67 and $0.1\textrm{g}\ F e_{3}0_{4}$ were dispersed into $25~\mathrm{mL}$ distilled $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ under sonication for $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ until a homogenous $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -ZIF-67 composite was formed. Thereafter, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ - ZIF-67 composite was slowly added to AmCs solution and stirred for $60\mathrm{min}$ . The AmCs solution containing $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -ZIF-67 was added drop by drop utilizing a syringe into $250\mathrm{mL}$ of a pre-prepared NaOH solution (5 $\%)$ . After curing for $15\mathrm{min}$ , the formed magnetic $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{ZIF-67}@\mathrm{AmCs}$ wet beads were collected by an external magnet, washed with distilled $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and dried at room temperature overnight. The magnetic $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F}$ - $67@\mathrm{{AmCs}}$ composite beads were synthesized with three mass ratios of ZIF-67 and AmCs; $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s1}$ beads (1.5:0.5), $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F}$ - $67@\mathrm{{AmCs2}}$ beads (1:1) and $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F{-}67@A m C s3}$ beads (0.5:1.5).
The schematic representation for the fabrication of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F}$ - $67@\mathrm{{AmCs3}}$ beads for the removal of $\mathrm{Cr(IV)}$ ions from aqueous solution is depicted in Fig. 1A.
## 2.6. Characterization
The fabricated samples were characterized by Fourier transforminfrared spectra (FTIR- Tensor II, Bruker) to prove their chemical composition. The thermal stability was examined by thermogravimetric analyzer and differential thermal analysis (TGA/DTA, Perkin-Elmer). Furthermore, scanning electron microscope and energy dispersive Xray micrscope (SEM- S4800, Hitachi) were utilized to investigate the morphology and the finger print of the fabricated samples, respectively. While, the cross-sectional structure was clarified by Stereo microscope (SM, Olympus). X-ray diffractometer (XRD- MAC Science M03XHF) was utilized to explore the crystal phase. Besides, the magnetic property was evaluated by vibrating sample magnetometer (VSM- Lake shore), while the surface charge was investigated by Zeta-sizer (Malvern-UK). The surface area was determined using the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller method (BET-Beckman coulter). The $\mathbf{x}$ -ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPSthermo scientific ESCALAB 250Xi VG) was utilized to investigate the elemental compostion of the samples.
## 2.7. Batch adsorption experiment
