Where, $\mathsf{C}_{0}$ and $\mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{t}}$ represent the initial $\mathrm{cr}$ (VI) concentration and its concentration at certain time, respectively. While, m and V represent the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{ZIF-67}@\mathrm{AmCs}$ beads mass and the $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ solution volume, respectively.
## 2.8. Recyclability test
From the economical spot, one of the most crucial characteristics that should be provided in the selected adsorbent is the possession of a good recycling property. Therefore, a recyclability test was conducted for seven adsorption-desorption cycles to assess the recycling charac­ teristic of the fabricated magnetic $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F-67}@\mathrm{AmCs}$ composite beads. After each adsorption process, the magnetic $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F}.$ - $67@\mathrm{{AmCs}}$ composite beads were collected by an external magnet, and then soaked into $50~\mathrm{mL}$ mixture solution (methanol/NaCl) and stirred gently for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ to assure that all adsorbed $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ ions were leached. Next, the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{ZIF-67}@\mathrm{AmCs}$ beads were examined in the sequent adsorp­ tion runs.
## 3. Result and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of the fabricated magnetic $F e_{3}O_{4}.$ /ZIF-67@AmCs3 beads
## 3.1.1. FTIR
Fig. 1B depicts FTIR spectra of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4},$ AmCs, ZIF-67 and $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F}$ - $67@\mathrm{{AmCs3}}$ beads before and after adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ ions. For pure $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , the two bands at 557 and $1405~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are ascribed to Fe–O stretching (Quy et al., 2013). Furthermore, the band at $892~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is corresponded to OH vibrating mode, while the absorption band at $1639\mathrm{\bar{cm}}^{-1}$ is related to OH bending mode (Stoia, Istratie, & Pa˘curariu, 2016). The broad band around $3437~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is assigned to OH stretching vibration. On the other hand, FTIR spectrum of AmCs reveals broad bands at 1062 and $3441~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ which are attributed to stretching vibration of $\mathsf{C-N}$ and $\mathrm{O-H}$ , respectively (Asiabi et al., 2017). In addi­ tion, the bands at 1402 and $2907\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are ascribed to C–H vibrating as well as the band at $1619~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is assigned to $\tt N-H$ bending (Travlou et al., 2013b). The bands at 2216 and $2901~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are attributed to C–OH stretching and $\mathrm{CH}_{2}$ stretching vibration, respectively (Yousefian & Rafiee, 2020). The spectrum of ZIF-67 shows the distinguishing band of Co-N bond stretching vibration at $413\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , asserting the successfully fabrication of ZIF-67 (Zhao et al., 2020). The two bands at 751 and $1576~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are assigned to $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}$ stretching vibration, while the band at $1303~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is corresponded to $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{C}$ stretching. Besides, the peak at 995 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ is related to $\mathsf{C-N}$ bending vibration, as well as, the existence of $\mathsf{C-N}$ stretching vibration is proved by the appearance of a band at $1144~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (Yoon, Calvo, & So, 2019). The band at $1429~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is attributed to $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ bending vibration, while the two bands at 2936 and $3135~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are assigned to C–H of aromatic ring and aliphatic hydro­ carbon, respectively (Ediati, Elfianuar, Santoso, Sulistiono, & Nadjib, 2019). Additionally, the band at $3448~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is most likely due to $\scriptstyle\mathrm{O-H}$ and $\tt N-H$ stretching vibration, while the band at $3616~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is ascribed to free $\scriptstyle\mathrm{O-H}$ stretching vibration (Du et al., 2017). The spectrum of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F{-}67@A m C s3}$ beads shows the distinctive peaks belong to the components of the beads, while the spectrum of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ beads after adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ ions reveals a new peak at $887~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ which is related to $\mathrm{CrO}_{4}$ tetrahedron stretching vibration (Zhu et al., 2017). Furthermore, the peak at $1147~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ belongs to $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{N}$ of imid­ azole ring disappeared after the adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ , implying the for­ mation of a chemical bond between $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ ions and ZIF-67.
## 3.1.2. XRD
XRD patterns (Fig. 1C) show the characteristic peaks of ZIF-67 at $2\uptheta=7.2^{\circ}$ , $10.2^{\circ}$ , $12.6^{\circ}$ , $14.5^{\circ}$ , $16.3^{\circ}$ , $18.01^{\circ}$ , $22^{\circ}$ , $24.4^{\circ}$ and $26.6^{\circ}$ which are corresponded to (011), (002), (112), (022), (013), (222), (114), (233) and (134) planes, respectively, asserting the successful synthesis of ZIF-67 with no impurities (Yan, Hu, Chen, Zhang, & Zhou, 2017; Yang, Lu et al., 2018). Besides, the sharpness of the diffraction peaks that reflects the high crystallinity of the as-synthesized ZIF-67 (Li, Gao, Ai, & Jiang, 2015). On the other hand, XRD pattern of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ beads shows the distinguishing peaks of ZIF-67, but with lower intensity than the pure ZIF-67. Furthermore, the presence of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ was recog­ nized by the appearance of the new diffraction peaks at $2\Theta=30.01^{\circ}$ ,
$35.55^{\circ}$ , $43.04^{\circ}$ , $54.83^{\circ}$ , $57.15^{\circ}$ and $62.64^{\circ}$ which are assigned to (220), (311), (400), (422), (511), and (440) planes, respectively (Travlou et al., 2013a).
## 3.1.3. VSM
The magnetic characteristics of the fabricated materials were assessed by VSM analysis. The hysteresis loops of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F}.$ - $67@\mathrm{{AmCs3}}$ beads are represented in Fig. 1D. It was found that both $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ beads possess soft-ferromagnetic behavior since their coericivity are 188.96 and $89.88\mathrm{~G~}$ , respectively. Furthermore, a significant decrease in the saturation magnetization was detected in case of the pristine $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ ( $43.59\mathrm{\emu/g}$ ) compared to the asfabricated $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ beads $(10.90\mathrm{\emu/g})$ which could be attributed to the combination of low amount of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ with the nonmagnetic ZIF-67@AmCs.
## 3.1.4. SEM
SEM images of pure AmCs beads (Fig. 2A, B) reveal spherical beadslike moon with a few wrinkles, while ZIF-67 particles (Fig. 2C, D) showed irregular surface with some aggregation which seem like lumps. On the other hand, SEM images of the developed $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ composite beads (Fig. 2E, F) exhibited non-spherical shape with a wrinkled coarse surface owing to the polarity difference between com­ posite composition (AmCs, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and ZIF-67). Fig. 2G clarifies that the fabricated $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ composite beads are almost uniform with an average size of $1.65\mathrm{mm}$ . Fig. 2H represents a stereo microscope image of the cross-sectional $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F{-}67@A m C s3}$ composite beads. Stereo microscope image shows irregular core surrounded by a double shell, suggesting the core-dual shell structure of the fabricated $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\$ ZIF-67@AmCs3 composite beads. Besides, digital photo of the cutted beads confirms the core-shell structure of the fabricated beads (Fig. 2I). The elemental composition of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ composite beads were confirmed by SEM-EDX (Fig. 2J). The results point out that the fabricated composite beads contain $38.00\ \%$ carbon, $9.36~\%$ nitrogen, $44.93\%$ oxygen, $1.37\%$ Fe and $6.34\%$ cobalt.
## 3.1.5. BET surface area
The BET surface area and pore size distribution of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F}.$ - $67@\mathrm{{AmCs3}}$ beads before and after adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ were determined using ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms (Fig. 3A). It is obvious that there is a sharp increase at relatively low pressure $(\mathrm{P/P_{0}<}$ $0.05\mathrm{atm}\dot{}$ ), confirming the microporous structure of the magnetic beads. Furthermore, both pure and Cr-loaded $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ beads represent type-IV isotherm with H4 hysteresis loop, revealing the exis­ tence of mesopores. The $\ensuremath{s}_{\mathrm{BET}}$ surface area and the total pore volume of pure $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ beads were found to be $220.76~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and $0.0124~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g},$ , respectively. However, after the adsorption process both SBET surface area and total pore volume of Cr-loaded $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F}.$ - $67@\mathrm{{AmCs3}}$ beads had decreased to $178.28\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and $0.00908~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}.$ , respectively. This is an expected behavior due to the agglomeration of adsorbate, causing the blockage of some micropores and mesopores. The BJH pore size distributions showed that the pores were narrowly distributed between $1\ \mathrm{nm}$ and $7\ \mathrm{nm}$ , reflecting the uniformity of pore structure in $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ beads. Besides, the difference in the intensities of peaks before and after adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr(IV)}$ reflecting the decrease in $\ensuremath{S_{\mathrm{BET}}}$ surface area as a result of blockage of some pores upon the adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr(IV)}$ ions (Liu et al., 2016).
## 3.1.6. TGA
Fig. 3B, C depict the thermal behaviors of the pristine ZIF-67 and $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F{-}67@A m C s3}$ composite beads. TG curve of ZIF-67 figures out three stages of weight loss; the first stage between 30 and $289^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ which is assigned to the elimination of moisture, accompanied with an endo­ thermic peak at $196^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in DTA curve. In addition, the second stage be­ tween 289 and $424^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is attributed to the removal of DMF, while the third one between 538 and $639^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is due to ligand collapse. One the other hand, TG curve of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ composite beads shows one more weight loss stage compared to the pristine ZIF-67 between 220 and $360^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ which is most likely due to AmCs degradation (Liang et al., 2019). It was found that the total weight loss of pristine ZIF-67 and
Fe3O4/ZIF-67@AmCs3 composite beads is about 80 and $73.3\mathrm{~\}0\%.$ respectively, reflecting one more benefit to the fabricated magnetic MOF beads that enhanced the thermal behavior compared to the pristine MOF.
## 3.2. Adsorption of Cr (VI) onto $F e_{3}O_{4}$ /ZIF-67@AmCs composite beads
To evaluate the adsorption performance of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , AmCs, ZIF-67, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\mathrm{Amcs}.$ , $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–F e_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s1_{\Omega}}$ $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F}.$ - $67@\mathrm{{AmCs2}}$ and $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ towards the removal of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ comparative test was conducted by soaking $_{0.01\mathrm{g}}$ of each adsorbent into $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ $\mathrm{\bar{20}\ m L}$ , $50~\mathrm{mg/L}]$ at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ Fig. 4A depicts that the adsorption capacity increased according to the order $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ beads $(71.35~\mathrm{~mg/g)}$ $>$ $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F{-}67@A m C s2}$ beads $(62.08\mathrm{~\mg/g)~\>~}$ $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}@\mathrm{AmCs}$ beads $(58.14~\mathrm{mg/g})>$ AmCs $[53.65~\mathrm{mg/g})~>~\mathrm{ZIF}{-}67$ $(44.63~\mathrm{mg/g})\ >\ \mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F-67}@\mathrm{AmCs1}$ beads $\mathrm{(35.76~m/g)~>~Fe_{3}O_{4}}$ $(14.55~\mathrm{mg/g})$ . This observation could be attributed to the synergetic effect between $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , AmCs and ZIF-67. Furthermore, an increase in the adsorption capacity with the increasing in the dose of AmCs which may be ascribed to the increase in the positive charges on the beads surface resulting from the presence of extra amine groups. As a result, the attraction forces between the positively charged $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{ZIF-67}@\mathrm{AmCs}$ beads and the anionic $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ ions increased and the adsorption capacity increased subsequently. Based on the above mentioned, $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F}.$ - $67@\mathrm{{AmCs3}}$ composite beads was picked out for the subsequent experiments.
## 3.2.1. Impact of pH
Undoubtedly, pH takes control of the form of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ in the aqueous solution where it presents as $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}0_{7}^{2-}$ and $\mathrm{HCrO}_{4}^{-}$ at pH range 2–6, while $\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{2-}$ is a predominant form at $\mathrm{pH}>6$ (Dehghani et al., 2016). Fig. 4B implies the impact of the increasing in pH solution from 2 to 9 on the adsorption capacity and the removal percentage of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ . It is obvious that the highest removal efficiency of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ onto $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I}.$ ­ $\mathbf{F}{\cdot}67@\mathbf{Am}\mathbf{C}s3$ beads at $\mathsf{p H}2$ . This behavior could be assigned to the protonation of $\mathrm{{NH}}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ at low $\mathsf{p H}$ that charges the surface of the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F{-}67@A m C s3}$ beads with a positive charge, resulting in an in­ crease in the electrostatic attraction force between the positively charged $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ beads and the anionic $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ ions. Furthermore, ${\mathsf{C o}}^{+2}$ of ZIF-67 competes with $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ to attract the anionic Cr (VI), resulting in an increase in the adsorption capacity (Yang, Ren et al., 2018). On the other hand, increasing pH from 2 to 5 causes a slight decrease in the removal percentage from 77.39 to $71.53\%$ accompanied with a decrease in the adsorption capacity from 71.50 to $67.74~\mathrm{mg/g}$ . This expected behavior is attributed to the decrease in the protonation of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ group with raising $\mathsf{p H}$ solution (Omer et al., 2019). However, beyond $\mathrm{pH}5$ there is a dramatic decrease in the removal percentage from 71.53 to $44.37\%$ as well as the adsorption capacity from 67.69 to $43.98\mathrm{~\mg/g}$ of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ onto $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ beads due the deprotonation of $\mathrm{{NH}}_{2}$ at higher pH values. Consequently, the electro­ static interaction between $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F{-}}67@\mathrm{AmCs3}$ beads and $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ de­ creases sharply (Parlayıcı & Pehlivan, 2019). This result was asserted using zeta potential analysis (Fig. 4C) since the highest surface charge of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F{-}67@A m C s3}$ beads $(51.2\mathrm{\mv})$ was found to be at $\mathrm{pH}2 $ . Furthermore, the $\mathrm{\pH_{\mathrm{pzc}}}$ of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F{-}67@A m C s3}$ beads was found to be 6.5, hence the surface charge of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ beads at $\mathrm{pH}<6.5$ is positive, suggesting a strong columbic interaction between the positively charged $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ beads and the anionic Cr (VI) ions. Conversely, at $\mathrm{pH}>6.5$ the surface of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F{-}67@A m C s3}$ beads is negatively charged, resulting in an electrostatic repulsion force between the negatively charged $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ beads and the anionic $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ ions.
## 3.2.2. Impact of $F e_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3$ beads dose
Fig. 5A points out the impact of the increasing in the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F}$ - $67@\mathrm{{AmCs3}}$ beads dose from 0.005 to $0.025g$ on the adsorption capacity and the removal percentage of Cr (VI). It is apparent that the increase in the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ beads dose leads to a decrease in the adsorption capacity from 89.77 to $35.40\mathrm{mg/g}$ which may be assigned to the aggregation of the particles. On the contrary, the removal percentage increases from 44.85 to $98.16\ \mathrm{~\}96\$ with increasing the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F}.$ - $67@\mathrm{{AmCs3}}$ beads dose which may be attributed to increase the number of active sites on the beads surface (El Bestawy et al., 2020).
## 3.2.3. Impact of initial concentration
Fig. 5B denotes that the adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ onto $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\$ $\mathrm{ZIF-}67@\mathrm{AmCs3}$ beads increases from 48.80 to $118.27~\mathrm{mg/g}$ with the increase in the initial concentration of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ from 25 to $100\mathrm{mg/L}$ . This anticipated behavior can be attributed to the increasing in the driving force with increasing initial concentration of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ that withstands the mass transfer resistance of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ ions to reach the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F}.$ - $67@\mathrm{{AmCs3}}$ beads surface. Contrariwise, there is a decrease in the removal percentage of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ from 97.41 to $57.47\%$ with the increase in the initial $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ concentration (Fig. 5C) which may be due to the insufficiency of the active sites that required for adsorption of high concentration of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ (Eltaweil, Abd El-Monaem, El-Subruiti, Abd El-Latif, & Omer, 2020).
## 3.2.4. Impact of temperature
Fig. 5D outlines the impact of increasing temperature on the effi­ ciency of the adsorption process of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ onto $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ beads. It is obvious that increasing temperature from $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to $35^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ has a good leverage on the process efficiency since both adsorption capacity and removal percentage increased from $71.35\mathrm{\mg/g}$ and $77.20~\%$ to $75.56~\mathrm{mg/g}$ and $82.35\ \%$ , respectively. This behavior can be explained by increasing in $\mathrm{Cr(IV)}$ ions motion toward $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ beads upon increasing the system temperature (Omer et al., 2019). However, further rise in the temperature beyond $35~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ causes to a dramatic decrease in both adsorption capacity and removal percentage from $75.56\mathrm{\mg/g}$ and $82.35~\%$ to $58.12~\mathrm{mg/g}$ and $61.03~\%$ , respectively, which may be attributed to the increasing in the thermal energy of the $\mathrm{cr}$ (VI) ions that leads to desorption (Samuel, EA Abigail, & Ramalingam, 2015).
## 3.3. Adsorption isotherms
Adsorption isotherms were sufficiently studied to figure out the sort of interaction between $\mathbf{Cr(VI)}$ and $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ beads in the designed adsorption system. The adsorption data were analyzed by various isotherm models including; Langmuir, Freundlich, Temkin and Dubinin-Radushkevich (D-R). The linear forms of these applied models are listed in Table S1.
Fig. 6 depicts the linear plots of Langmuir, Freundlich, Temkin and
D-R isotherm models. The $\scriptstyle\mathrm{\mathsf{R}}^{2}$ values (Table 1) point out that the adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ onto $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F{-}67@A m C s3}$ composite beads fol­ lows Langmuir (0.986) and Freundlich (0.983) compared to Temkin (0.952) and D-R (0.863).
From Langmuir model; the maximum adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ onto $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F{-}67@A m C s3}$ beads is $119.05~\mathrm{{\mg/g}}$ . Besides, the computed values of the dimensionless separation factor $\begin{array}{r}{(\mathbb{R}_{\mathrm{L}}=\frac{1}{1+\mathrm{b}\mathbb{C}_{0}})}\end{array}$ fall between 0–1 (Table S3), indicating the favorability for the uptake of $\mathrm{cr}$ (VI) onto $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ beads. Otherwise, the Preference for this uptake process was confirmed by the computed Freundlich constant $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{(n=4.679)}}\end{array}$ . Temkin model points out that the uptake of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ onto $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ beads takes place via physical adsorption, this result is agreed with D-R model since the bonding energy $\mathrm{(E=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2K_{\mathrm{ad}}}})}$ was found to be less than $8\mathrm{KJ/mol}$ (Erhayem, Al-Tohami, Mohamed, & Ahmida, 2015). Generally, physical adsorption occurs via weak van der Waals interactions, hence the uptake of $\mathbf{Cr(VI)}$ onto $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I}.$ ­ F-67@AmCs3 beads needs low adsorption energy (Araújo et al., 2018).
## 3.4. Adsorption kinetics
To fully understand the uptake mechanism of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ onto $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F}$ - $67@\mathrm{{AmCs3}}$ composite beads, the experimental data were inspected by Pseudo 1st order, Pseudo 2nd order as well as Elovich model. The linear forms of these models are summarized in Table (S2).
Fig. 7(A, B and C) represents the plots of the applied kinetic models; Pseudo 1st order, Pseudo 2nd order and Elovich. It is apparent from the ${\tt R}^{2}$ values (Table 2) that pseudo 2nd order is the best kinetic model to represent the uptake process of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ onto $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ beads. Moreover, the analogy between the calculated $\mathsf{q}$ values from pseudo 2nd order and the actual experimental values is one more evi­ dence proves that pseudo 2nd order is the most suitable model compared to the other applied models. Besides, the computed $\upalpha$ and $\upbeta$ values from Elovich model confirm that the rate of adsorption is higher than desorption where the $\upalpha$ values are higher than $\upbeta$ values (Ahamad, Singh, Baruah, Choudhury, & Sharma, 2018).
## 3.5. Impact of coexisting ions
Wastewater contains tremendous other ions that compete forcefully with $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ for the adsorption sites of the fabricated $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F}.$ - $67@\mathrm{{AmCs3}}$ composite beads. Subsequently, the adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F{-}67@A m C s3}$ composite beads towards $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ ions directly declines. Fig. 7D and E depicts the impact of some selected ions; cations $\mathrm{{(Na^{+}}}$ , $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{K}^{+}\mathrm{\dot{\Omega}}$ ) and anions $(\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ , $S O_{4}^{2-}$ , $\ N O_{3^{-}}$ ) on the adsorption ca­ pacity of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ ions onto $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ composite beads. It is obvious that the presence of coexisting cations causes a slight decrease in the adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ ions onto $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ composite beads, asserting a little competitive ability of the cations with $\mathbf{Cr(VI)}$ ions (Deng et al., 2020). This anticipated behavior most likely due to the electrostatic repulsion force between $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ composite beads that positively charged at low pH and the coexisting cations. Contrariwise, the existence of coexisting anions causes a significant decrease in the adsorptive removal of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ ions, proving the great competition of the coexisting anions with $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ for the active sites on the surface of the beads. This strong competitive ability of anions could be due to their negative charge that spontaneously attracts to the positively charged $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ composite beads. The results show that the presence of $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ and $\mathsf{N O}_{3}^{-}$ causes an insignificant decrease in the adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ ions onto the beads which could be due to that Cl- and $\mathsf{N O}_{3}^{-}$ are low-affinity ligands, so they form weak outer-sphere complexes with the surface of the beads. On contrary, $s0_{4}^{2\cdot}$ forms inner and outer sphere composites that renders a part of the active sites of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ composite beads occupied with $s0_{4}^{2\cdot}$ , so the adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ ions sharply decreases in the presence of the coexisting $S0_{4}^{2-}$ (Xiang et al., 2021).
## 3.6. Reusability
To evaluate the renewability of the fabricated magnetic $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F}$ - $67@\mathrm{{AmCs3}}$ composite beads to reuse many times for the removal of Cr (VI) ions, seven adsorption/desorption cycles were accomplished. Fig. 7F indicates that the removal powerful of the developed adsorbent still exceeded $60\%$ after the seventh cycle with a maximum adsorption capacity reached $73.05~\mathrm{mg/g}$ . These observations confirm the excellent recycling property of the fabricated magnetic $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ beads.
## 3.7. Comparison with other adsorbents
Table 3 depicts a comparison between the fabricated magnetic $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ composite beads and adsorbents reported in previous studies (Guo, Li, & Wang, 2019; Karthik & Meenakshi, 2015; Li et al., 2015; Omer et al., 2019; Shahrak, Ghahramaninezhad, & Eydi­ farash, 2017; Zhang et al., 2015; Zhu et al., 2017). It is obvious that $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ composite beads possess excellent adsorption capacity toward $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ . Furthermore, it is worthy to mention that the adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ onto $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ composite beads is a fast process with an equilibrium time of $60\ \mathrm{\min}$ . This remarkable adsorption behavior of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ composite beads may be due to the synergistic effect between $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , ZIF-67 and AmCs. Besides, the presence of extra amine groups on the surface of the beads that provide abundance of the positive charges on the surface of the beads (surface charge $=51.2\mathrm{\mv}$ at $\mathsf{p H}=2\mathsf{\cdot}$ ), leading to strong columbic interaction between the positively charged $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ composite beads and the anionic $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ ions.
## 3.8. Adsorption mechanism
XPS wide-spectrum 1 (Fig. 8A) asserts the successful fabrication of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F67}$ $\ @\mathrm{{AmCs3}}$ composite beads as it confirms that the beads consist of C, O, N, Fe and Co which well agrees with SEM-EDX result. In addition, spectrum 2 (Fig. 8A) points out occurring the adsorption of Cr (VI) ions onto $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F{-}67@A m C s3}$ composite beads since the distinctive peaks of $\mathrm{cr}$ ions is clearly emerged. The high-resolution spectrum of $\mathtt{C r2p}$ (Fig. 8B) suggests the reduction of the adsorbed Cr (VI) to $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{III})$ since the peak at the binding energy of $578.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ is cor­ responded to $\mathrm{Cr(VI)2p}_{3/2}$ , while the peaks at the binding energy of 586.9 and $577.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ are assigned to $\mathrm{Cr(III)2p_{1/2}}$ and $\mathrm{Cr(III)2p_{3/2}},$ , respectively (Wang, Zhang, & Lv, 2019). Besides, Fig. 8C, D illustrates the state of distributed amino groups on the surface of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ composite beads before and after the adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ ions. The high-resolution spectrum of N1s before the $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ ions adsorption fig­ ures out the peaks at 400.02 and $398.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ which are attributed to NH and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , respectively. While the spectrum of N1s after the adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ shows a new peak at $401.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ which is ascribed to $\mathrm{NH}_{3}^{+}$ , indi­ cating the protonation of the amine group during the adsorption process (Omer et al., 2019). We can hypothesize that the adsorption mechanism of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ ions onto $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ composite beads occurs via three steps; at first, the distributed amino groups on the surface of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F–67@A m C s3}$ composite beads are converted to the protonated form $\mathrm{(NH}_{3}^{+})$ ) at low pH. Subsequently, the surface of the beads is charged by a positive charge, leading to a strong columbic interaction between the positively charged beads and the anionic $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ . Secondly, the electron-donner groups on the surface of the beads such as $\boldsymbol{(\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ and OH) reduce the adsorbed $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ to $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{III})$ . The third step involves the formation of a coordinate–covalent bond between $\mathrm{Cr(III)}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/.$ ZIF-67@AmCs3 composite beads as shown in the following Eqs.
$$
\mathrm{HCrO^{-}}+7\mathrm{H^{+}}+3\mathrm{e^{-}}\rightarrow\mathrm{Cr^{3+}}+4\mathrm{H_{2}O}
$$
$$
\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}^{2-}+14\mathrm{H}^{+}+6\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow2\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}+7\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}
$$
