## 4. Conclusion
In sum, our study has proposed a sophisticated strategy to provide new reusable adsorbent based on $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -decorated ZIF-67@AmCs com­ posite beads for the adsorptive removal of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ ions. The results dis­ played that the developed adsorbent exposed a fast adsorption rate towards $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ ions, which reached the equilibrium within $60\ \mathrm{min}$ . Besides, the fabricated $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{ZIF-67}@\mathrm{AmCs}$ composite beads recorded a maximum adsorption capacity of $119.05\mathrm{mg/g}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Furthermore, the results revealed that the adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ onto $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z I F}.$ - $67@\mathrm{{AmCs}}$ composite beads fitted Langmuir and Freundlich models, while the adsorption process followed the pseudo 2nd order kinetic model. The recyclability test proved the feasibility of our strategy to obtain reusable and efficient adsorbent toward $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ ions with acceptable adsorption properties.
